#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "b52808fa-1f08-53ac-a5e0-97dd05b1e57b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3552" :question/problem "Find the general solution to $2\\cos{x} + \\sqrt{2}= 0,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cos x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/5f32529ce4132c594d8a886222ff6f1a356eefa95fa4c7e1fd38b9bd4e3932a9.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,360)$ are $x=135^\\circ, 225^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation, where $n$ is an integer, is\n\n$$\nx = 135^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ, \\quad x=225^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/id #uuid "6831fce9-9c10-5c75-9431-529b852c9bed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3553" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\cot{x}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arctan \\left( 1 \\right)= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi.$  Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is: \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/id #uuid "5a599246-2feb-59fa-b859-3b9bd20c9e57" :question/math-academy-id "e-3554" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $3\\csc x+2\\sqrt{3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\csc x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =-\\dfrac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\sin x<   0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/0716b8ab50e525f453ae8bc690d404a150f2da98f0a90b6c8856ae9925e7c515.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in[0,2\\pi)$ are $x=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi, \\quad x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}+2n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "4457a33e-7022-53b2-bcad-f565ec762f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3555" :question/problem "The graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\sec{x}$ and the line $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ What is the general solution for the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/cae8d73bdf9a21e956c444db4b22bfc26e20b7a9d1593d7759fc119aa4c6a623.png)" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\sec x =\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nRecall that $\\sec{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\arccos \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)= 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos x >   0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions for $x\\in\\left[0^\\circ,360^\\circ\\right)$ are $x=45^\\circ, 315^\\circ.$ Therefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx = 45^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ , \\quad x=315^\\circ+n\\cdot 360^\\circ ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f57a4347-bc97-570b-9280-a2096a56a01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279181} {:answer/id #uuid "62381e7d-186a-5b8e-8a40-81cebd53b7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279182} {:answer/id #uuid "47d9d1c6-698d-5f42-acac-dfb81c096a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279183} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0b0fa3-18ee-5d53-985c-bcdb0188b7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279184} {:answer/id #uuid "d0134b8e-d525-5f1b-9a39-1bf27cb6f4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62381e7d-186a-5b8e-8a40-81cebd53b7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279182} :answer-field/id #uuid "5893dc9e-0809-506e-a0a5-b57241a0111d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db88ade5-9f00-5732-8750-043df145e23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120762" :question/problem "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eddafd-639f-5fbb-9ec9-69894b9bfbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279188} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee82f-3de3-5882-a2bc-2c650a649684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279189} {:answer/id #uuid "828afad4-4379-5fac-9e35-13a358b8962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={225}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279190} {:answer/id #uuid "919f5030-a559-5195-8acc-6dfe5f65fd20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279191} {:answer/id #uuid "32bb84c7-cb60-5118-8430-f4c62475fd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee82f-3de3-5882-a2bc-2c650a649684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279189} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b66743-d5b2-5446-853e-ba7c11915bf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d5d3d5-2893-5d8a-9d82-fbb4c7b2b493" :question/math-academy-id "q-120767" :question/problem "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x={\\theta }^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},$ where $\\theta \\in [0,360),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68ef848f-988b-509e-b1ea-454745e7c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279195} {:answer/id #uuid "efb3b8d0-3138-5e1c-a8df-9a609b115df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} {:answer/id #uuid "84751089-2a08-51d3-8812-3f0ab679e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279197} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8e1f9a-42f2-556c-aa43-495c2e2085af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279198} {:answer/id #uuid "f15317e3-a694-54f6-a42e-c7da42d2f0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84751089-2a08-51d3-8812-3f0ab679e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279197} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed16514b-be29-57e6-ae3b-2415d1f76a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a6f498d-68d3-5e27-80bb-d7db1ffb5627" :question/math-academy-id "q-120771" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/269d503189ab1027c3d8028542ef0c6a0e2f146aef0f7916691428866ce1b894.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c8aa9ec-0463-5918-94a5-92291773b24c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279202} {:answer/id #uuid "0d980e78-3a32-5720-9f23-e77976e1dc47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279203} {:answer/id #uuid "489ef6b8-1050-5d58-8ab4-662671b650df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279204} {:answer/id #uuid "e83abfb7-e0ac-5e31-889c-54ccc259ca33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279205} {:answer/id #uuid "c659cfa6-a5ec-5050-8b5f-4912effc74a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d980e78-3a32-5720-9f23-e77976e1dc47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279203} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e494bc-d874-599e-b5dc-daf8f7440f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ade59e51-4bbe-5c7e-8008-65e668e73bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-120772" :question/problem "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c85984a-1f15-58a2-81aa-427943d609f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279209} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb7e36c-c37a-541f-a135-2134449eecea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279210} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ddfe79-fee3-5514-bef2-88871050d039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279211} {:answer/id #uuid "a0eec919-8ae9-5564-b087-0fea785124d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279212} {:answer/id #uuid "31cc23f7-a9b8-5502-bc45-2e835d2741ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb7e36c-c37a-541f-a135-2134449eecea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279210} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f213d4-5b12-59c5-a54d-52ab166a824b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68f1f323-741d-52af-9831-144170cc842f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120777" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2060c21-ddea-55bd-8b5c-e82779e79659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279216} {:answer/id #uuid "444b1fe3-ea79-5a24-a3e9-e43318ce5d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279217} {:answer/id #uuid "88346a51-78fe-52ae-a33a-63b9fb270851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279218} {:answer/id #uuid "f19f48dd-1f3a-5899-899c-2e3a4f28cb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279219} {:answer/id #uuid "44c6b9c5-6af2-5294-83e7-894727dbdbbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88346a51-78fe-52ae-a33a-63b9fb270851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279218} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b06de0d-e1a9-5c3d-80c8-699131239fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bcc510d2-a371-51aa-8fd4-7a40a4663248" :question/math-academy-id "q-120791" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c27398934832fba39571fb74c9fa2f9ef80d5ff8ea27136f5353a67ea2cd9ae6.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/c78678fade9fd2a60003a0ab2cac0299ef1e7df9f6633b1fc6a97422e13dfc4d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e0318e-08ba-56e4-9438-d75356204d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279223} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb297cf-411a-5aa9-87b9-38b0663754f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279224} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8f29bf-8da0-55ce-b272-7c78f6aabb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279225} {:answer/id #uuid "68938b0f-1c2e-5b5e-adf2-90983c076056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279226} {:answer/id #uuid "d4947629-9022-5630-acc0-b93ac1ee7057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae8f29bf-8da0-55ce-b272-7c78f6aabb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279225} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f34c6-ecb0-553c-bf5f-d4dd8ce66e1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55f2eb2e-8042-5b14-9e44-35110ff3ed0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16227" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThe sine function has a period of $2\\pi .$ Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b379ac19-f5bf-5c36-a115-bcc67a69f9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279230} {:answer/id #uuid "2159f4d4-2cce-5e96-8965-57e31dd24a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279231} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dae689-92ab-5a8c-9c4c-c3136b392874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279232} {:answer/id #uuid "dce3cebc-22ea-5cd0-81bd-346489417e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279233} {:answer/id #uuid "e305d0d6-28ff-526f-b394-269f76b326c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e305d0d6-28ff-526f-b394-269f76b326c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279234} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f4fd28-a2dd-5c29-81e3-76be0d8560a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04c1e06c-4d7d-5329-b6b7-84f41d592325" :question/math-academy-id "q-16228" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation  $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/bf1efb64724fb0dfbf60cc14905e63ba867caa0bf0edd888b35fb554af97c856.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad76bd79-29ec-5162-b3b5-6767fbbd167f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098653} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2c5383-78cf-502a-b9b3-647a6546ef75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 90^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098654} {:answer/id #uuid "186d6335-deb2-5be6-ab27-0d972f0f25ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 30^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098655} {:answer/id #uuid "28f9c780-09a0-5072-9d82-dcd078517ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-30^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098656} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8719c2-5250-5d34-89aa-de9912ce2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8719c2-5250-5d34-89aa-de9912ce2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-60^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098657} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75cd6ad-07e3-55a6-8811-6d153734ac99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8aaf6f0-933e-536c-8ff9-c54f73295028" :question/math-academy-id "q-16229" :question/problem "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-{60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3907c9fa-acff-56a8-b049-708150e3c989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279237} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa036a-4909-50c5-99e7-b805fa560e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279238} {:answer/id #uuid "e439dd9a-7901-554e-8595-16083b0c9f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279239} {:answer/id #uuid "711034d0-1346-550d-ae03-cb593210bea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279240} {:answer/id #uuid "779d7901-3832-54cf-9e29-5de14bb837da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "779d7901-3832-54cf-9e29-5de14bb837da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279241} :answer-field/id #uuid "df08ff16-11f5-53e9-9812-f18963beb19b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9be36d7-894f-5e66-b4f9-bb115161d64e" :question/math-academy-id "q-16245" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/350458d29c8d214f44b175e28a6f43accd7ce064187cf755324cbe99d53eb7ad.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $y=-1.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-1.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e205850-a206-5b5f-986b-dd160dcab2f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279244} {:answer/id #uuid "60af553c-254a-54f9-88d0-8249814b220e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279245} {:answer/id #uuid "e0747963-4881-57cc-be3b-f00ffe6dea46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279246} {:answer/id #uuid "319d7db8-7a31-5621-a62d-455c83f8e080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279247} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e392c1-9a7d-56d7-a9e1-fce0b3d8a9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60af553c-254a-54f9-88d0-8249814b220e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279245} :answer-field/id #uuid "2907fab5-c6d5-57d6-9c57-15412f9309f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cd8eb71-b053-59d5-9b95-ce17acfec035" :question/math-academy-id "q-16246" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/f6e3ee6eed44a5ecb7e037e9cd8401a95b3e2a4640bd4454f60019f5bd9d1e67.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1944991c-8f78-5313-bb27-1ac2a458500e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279251} {:answer/id #uuid "2fde3a6f-f23a-5cbb-8c33-586059dc6ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279252} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b9b3ef-f658-54d5-9fa5-266be1fd5f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279253} {:answer/id #uuid "2eea1780-4f51-5fa9-a800-e56b95d564da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279254} {:answer/id #uuid "578edc55-f441-5963-ac06-23321c6061d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eea1780-4f51-5fa9-a800-e56b95d564da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279254} :answer-field/id #uuid "767469eb-9ad3-516e-800a-b7f6c7474235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef0aea1b-59b4-57dd-94ee-0eb39bb522ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-29979" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/355d3770234a7b0c9a65965d65ad3807adedc7e9c6c8a23ddbe25bb34a0dd5d0.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.$ \n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "740e410b-4cd9-5f46-94af-3837fa7f4e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740e410b-4cd9-5f46-94af-3837fa7f4e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279258} :answer-field/id #uuid "429a304f-6ece-5ee7-b928-8df4c03c7026" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279257} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381fa807-71e1-5192-baf8-f17f4a205dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "381fa807-71e1-5192-baf8-f17f4a205dcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b8ba98-2b0f-51d5-b72b-9fdd65e6e92f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279259} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb91fb69-58db-5781-a5a3-43e8f057f609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb91fb69-58db-5781-a5a3-43e8f057f609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279262} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c2f551-4fda-51ca-a403-09eda0306c84" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1f1c3b0-8cbe-5a8b-bae9-2d90f403a840" :question/math-academy-id "q-322283" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/5af1febb2c8dbf1b19ee64addd8f8ccdd5a30f9330d36ab800475fce9f6740c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ and $b=2\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79a113d-b76e-515f-b701-b716bc4a7db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279265} {:answer/id #uuid "84cbd265-e976-56c3-a05b-19061fbd80b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84cbd265-e976-56c3-a05b-19061fbd80b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279266} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78eb1b8-4cd6-5e5a-9516-d48b50c55109" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7036ba8-b346-5a11-849a-cd63de4371fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7036ba8-b346-5a11-849a-cd63de4371fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279268} :answer-field/id #uuid "08c10665-d925-55f1-a528-0ceb8a67786d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad4ceedb-3680-5594-963e-f88eaf71c43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad4ceedb-3680-5594-963e-f88eaf71c43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279270} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ae2c72-6abf-59fa-a60b-3744fa742d3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f27f08e8-ab1c-560d-bad3-e5b4ddec16eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-322656" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-6=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,{360}^{∘}),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{3\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n,\\,x={315}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=45\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ ${\\theta }_{2}=315\\,{\\,}^{∘},$ and $b=360\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae62f399-f21d-5324-819a-6d19ce9ceff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae62f399-f21d-5324-819a-6d19ce9ceff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279273} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3400d33-bfc5-5fa5-8f11-3684625039be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c326a8ed-ce09-5f2f-9aaa-e76d2eee33a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c326a8ed-ce09-5f2f-9aaa-e76d2eee33a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279275} :answer-field/id #uuid "912518c3-95dc-5978-ad30-9aecd58fb359" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a42d33e-966f-5790-be0b-d7a71cb4547f" :question/math-academy-id "q-323833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({15}^{∘})=2-\\sqrt{3}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(2-\\sqrt{3})={15}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1741e7d-df35-5e0f-9fac-564e954ede35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279278} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dd6d70-12e9-5d73-b847-1f7ec278b5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1741e7d-df35-5e0f-9fac-564e954ede35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279278} :answer-field/id #uuid "6953a8a7-3e6b-5313-96d5-d68098862784" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd2b3e3-600e-5c43-87db-089ab360f60e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd2b3e3-600e-5c43-87db-089ab360f60e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279281} :answer-field/id #uuid "3419f1f6-91eb-5a19-b9fb-ac12b81fbaef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2311362-a42c-55b7-8a4a-1b72a677fcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-323836" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9ee1abc-8012-5961-ab5a-30f63b4709a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9ee1abc-8012-5961-ab5a-30f63b4709a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279284} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0dd745-a6b1-5891-b1f7-6f119c4a0f68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cc414bc-0d9f-565e-af0a-ac05455a3868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cc414bc-0d9f-565e-af0a-ac05455a3868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279286} :answer-field/id #uuid "7024a22e-f0cc-5ff4-8b96-f913cb795b5e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef4ae1e3-38bc-5f9b-bb58-70f2ac357b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-323846" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing this as $x=\\theta +{180}^{∘}\\,n,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ we get\n\n$$\nx={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b57e17-a515-5f1c-8c07-30b4688a92fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279289} {:answer/id #uuid "1b05cdd7-c227-5fa2-9192-e4c9321631e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b05cdd7-c227-5fa2-9192-e4c9321631e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279290} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93d35b8-5820-545e-9fb6-8de12373185d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279288} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bc1b25-f930-520a-afcc-24ac64ddbc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58bc1b25-f930-520a-afcc-24ac64ddbc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279292} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37e995b-ec92-5afe-97ca-63af004855f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279291} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68070cdc-8f00-5169-af3b-cba7c8ae8484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68070cdc-8f00-5169-af3b-cba7c8ae8484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279294} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df63614-fd54-5a09-b46b-1b7f2a85f3c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f54ae505-1f6b-5a00-9a72-c2517a389f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-324056" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea7cf4b-ccc8-543a-a86e-d0cd67f54b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279297} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5cd8e4-3c5f-5dca-8ccb-3e18af1ac9d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279298} {:answer/id #uuid "e62fd867-b2c0-5895-a0e2-d0c277ec0afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279299} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bfd326-7f02-5247-9247-b51fced0a16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279300} {:answer/id #uuid "66a0e08b-3a52-5744-ad73-104b99462761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62fd867-b2c0-5895-a0e2-d0c277ec0afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279299} :answer-field/id #uuid "3423f75c-5bc3-5b0b-8f9d-41bad4fec7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6d74ee2-ab7f-5432-8051-c19d5ab9bb86" :question/math-academy-id "q-76736" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2=0?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $4$th quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979076/assets/87b3a90142f4c810433bd2c219382379fc508fa426567708abddb744f39771c2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWriting this as $x=\\theta +2n\\pi ,$ where $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4df616c-96da-5b6d-8c2c-26c92ba4212d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279304} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3b4828-a6c4-5f02-a51c-5bafa8e95bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279305} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0e2181-cf32-55ab-aa54-b5eb6e215c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279306} {:answer/id #uuid "b4255686-8b1e-5375-9d4e-852d4f54d76e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279307} {:answer/id #uuid "495b4bb4-0c6b-5218-9e1b-15c6e26ebdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4df616c-96da-5b6d-8c2c-26c92ba4212d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18c5875-e4c2-58b3-8da7-69e0fdcf658f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4220d4aa-1e88-5133-8caa-5c466911c81d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76741" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})=1+\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(\\sqrt{2}+1)=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+n\\pi .\n$$"}]}
